From 4d389976660d5044905db20e675b07685e03e986 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arya Alikhani <alexanderaryaalikhani@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 10:13:44 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 16/17] boringssl: optionally send EMS and RI in TLS 1.3-only
 hellos

Firefox's QUIC ClientHello carries an empty extended_master_secret and
renegotiation_info with an empty renegotiated_connection, although
QUIC only negotiates TLS 1.3. NSS sends both whenever they are enabled,
whatever its version range (ssl3_SendExtendedMasterSecretXtn,
ssl3_SendRenegotiationInfoXtn), and Firefox enables both. BoringSSL
omits both when the client's minimum version is TLS 1.3.

Add SSL_CTX_set_tls12_extensions_in_tls13_client_hello and its SSL
variant. When enabled, a client whose minimum version is TLS 1.3 sends
both extensions as a TLS 1.2-capable client would, over TLS and QUIC.
One switch covers both because NSS sends them together. The default is
unchanged.

ClientHelloInner still omits them. It cannot negotiate TLS 1.2, and
NSS drops extensions that are unknown to TLS 1.3 when it builds the
inner hello (tls13_ConstructInnerExtensionsFromOuter), so with real
ECH only ClientHelloOuter carries them. SSL_OP_NO_RENEGOTIATION still
omits renegotiation_info. A server that echoes either extension in
TLS 1.3 is still rejected, as NSS does; ssl_test checks this for an
echoed extended_master_secret. BoringSSL servers ignore both at
TLS 1.3.
